      "country": "IND",
      "split_group": "unsc-meeting:8926:2021-12-13",
      "output": "India’s 9 December 2021 statement to the UN Security Council clarifies its position on climate change and security, countering the analyst’s characterization that India “denied that climate change could exacerbate conflict.” India acknowledged that climate change has impacted people’s lives and “exacerbated conflicts in many places,” but emphasized that “viewing conflicts only through the prism of climate change presents a misleading perspective.” It cited the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), noting that “the effect of climate variability on violence is contested” and there is “no clear scientific pronouncement directly equating climate change with security concerns.” India argued that climate matters should be addressed under the UNFCCC, not the Security Council, to avoid disrupting the inclusive, consensus-driven process of the climate regime. The country expressed support for the Glasgow Climate Pact and reaffirmed its commitment to the Paris Agreement, including its 2030 and 2070 climate targets. India’s institutional preference is to maintain the existing UNFCCC framework for climate action, rather than creating a new Security Council mechanism. (S/PV.8923, 2021-12-09)",
